WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.080
Imagine you're in a consulting interview.

00:00:02.550 --> 00:00:05.830
Your interviewer tells you a restaurant's profits are declining.

00:00:06.280 --> 00:00:07.400
How would you investigate?

00:00:07.970 --> 00:00:11.430
Instead of jumping to solutions, let's break the problem down.

00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:14.610
Profit equals revenue minus costs.

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:20.140
If profits decline, revenue may be falling, costs may be rising, or both.

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:24.070
This relationship forms the foundation of a profitability tree.

00:00:24.440 --> 00:00:27.560
Revenue equals price multiplied by volume.

00:00:28.010 --> 00:00:30.800
For a restaurant, that's the average price per meal

00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:33.130
multiplied by the number of meals sold.

00:00:33.350 --> 00:00:38.760
If revenue declines, we investigate whether prices decreased or fewer meals were sold.

00:00:39.070 --> 00:00:42.500
Costs can be divided into fixed and variable costs.

00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:47.070
Fixed costs, like rent, generally remain constant in the short term.

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:51.040
Variable costs, like ingredients, change with sales volume.

00:00:51.350 --> 00:00:54.250
A restaurant sells two thousand meals at ten dollars each,

00:00:54.150 --> 00:00:57.080
generating twenty thousand dollars in revenue.

00:00:57.300 --> 00:01:02.910
Each meal costs four dollars to produce, creating eight thousand dollars in variable costs.

00:01:03.110 --> 00:01:08.540
Add six thousand dollars in fixed costs, and total costs reach fourteen thousand dollars.

00:01:08.930 --> 00:01:10.850
Profit is six thousand dollars.

00:01:11.130 --> 00:01:14.000
Now sales fall to eighteen hundred meals.

00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:16.760
Revenue drops to eighteen thousand dollars.

00:01:17.130 --> 00:01:22.890
Variable costs fall to seven thousand two hundred dollars, while fixed costs remain unchanged.

00:01:23.310 --> 00:01:26.130
Profit becomes four thousand eight hundred dollars.

00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:28.790
The main driver is lower sales volume.

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:32.350
A profitability tree helps you structure complex problems,

00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:35.680
identify their drivers, and focus your analysis.

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:38.440
A great framework isn't just a diagram.

00:01:38.590 --> 00:01:41.010
It's a tool for asking better questions.

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:43.440
Now it's your turn to practice.

